Relevant columns, their potential usage & hints for later joins/filters  

• GITHUB_REPOS_DATE.YEAR."_2023"  
  – "type"              : filter for 'PullRequestEvent' only  
  – "created_at"        : restrict to events that happened on 2023‑01‑18  
  – "repo"              : VARIANT; contains the repository’s full name ("name" element) and id – use it to join to the languages table  
  – "id" (event id)     : may help for ad‑hoc inspection / ordering  

• GITHUB_REPOS_DATE.GITHUB_REPOS."LANGUAGES"  
  – "repo_name"         : matches the "name" field extracted from the YEAR table  
  – "language"          : VARIANT; GitHub returns a map of language → bytes ‑ flatten it, rank by bytes and keep the largest (the primary language)  

The basic conditions you will typically need  

1. "type" = 'PullRequestEvent'  
2. "created_at" is on 2023‑01‑18 (you can first inspect the numeric layout, then bracket the range for that day)  
3. Group by primary language and keep languages whose total PR‑event count ≥ 100  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL snippets (LIMIT 20 for demonstration) that progress from “peeking” at the raw data up to assembling everything you’ll need.  
Remember: keep every identifier inside double quotes.

```sql
